enhanced_concept.explanation:
Memory management's vital. Manual methods (malloc/free in C) risk leaks/dangling pointers. GC automates reclamation. In x86-64, memory is addressed using 64-bit virtual addresses. GCC/Clang on Linux use the x86-64 ABI. Memory layout follows alignment rules. `struct {char a; int b;}` has padding after `a` to align `b` (usually 4 bytes). Consider:
```c
int *ptr = malloc(sizeof(int));
*ptr = 10;
// Later, if ptr is lost, memory is leaked.
```
A GC would reclaim this. Register `%rax` often holds return values (e.g., from `malloc`). `movq` moves data between registers/memory. Address 0x7ffc24001000 might hold `ptr`. `movl $10, (%rax)` would store 10 at the address in `%rax`. Memory is organized into segments (code, data, stack, heap). `free(ptr)` returns memory; the heap manager (part of the C runtime) tracks free blocks. GC implementations vary (mark-and-sweep is common).

