enhanced_concept.explanation:
`setjmp`/`longjmp` enable non-local jumps. `setjmp(jmp_buf env)` saves the current execution context (stack pointer `%rsp`, instruction pointer `%rip`, register state) into `env`. It returns 0. `longjmp(jmp_buf env, int val)` restores the context from `env`, effectively returning to the `setjmp` call site. However, `longjmp` returns `val` (or 1 if `val` is 0).

x86-64 Assembly (GCC/Clang, Linux): `setjmp` typically saves `%rbx`, `%rsp`, `%rbp`, `%r12`, `%r13`, `%r14`, `%r15`, `%rip` and possibly floating point registers (depending on compiler/flags) into the `jmp_buf`.  `longjmp` restores these registers.  The stack pointer `%rsp` is critical; incorrect restoration leads to crashes.

Memory Layout (`jmp_buf` - implementation defined): The `jmp_buf` is an array of `long` (typically 8 bytes on x86-64). The exact order and meaning of elements are compiler-specific.  Commonly: `env[0]` might store `%rbx`, `env[1]` `%rsp`, `env[2]` `%rbp`, `env[3]` `%r12`, `env[4]` `%r13`, `env[5]` `%r14`, `env[6]` `%r15`, `env[7]` `%rip`. Padding may exist for alignment.  Accessing `jmp_buf` directly is generally unsafe and discouraged; rely on `setjmp`/`longjmp`.

Example:

```c
#include <setjmp.h>
#include <stdio.h>

jmp_buf env;

void func(int i) {
  if (i > 3) {
    printf("Longjmping with value: %d\n", i);
    longjmp(env, i); // Jump back to setjmp
  }
  printf("func(%d)\n", i);
}

int main() {
  int val = setjmp(env);
  if (val == 0) {
    printf("Setjmp returned 0\n");
    func(1);
    func(2);
    func(4); // Triggers longjmp
  } else {
    printf("Longjmp returned: %d\n", val);
  }
  return 0;
}
```

If `setjmp` returns 0, the program proceeds normally. When `func(4)` is called, `longjmp(env, 4)` is executed. This unwinds the stack back to the point where `setjmp` was called in `main`. The `setjmp` then returns 4.  The "Longjmp returned: 4" message is printed. The stack is restored to the state it was in when `setjmp` was called, except for the return value.

